      "content": "the Bayesian approach is a solid starting point for isolating the signal. by treating the \"spike\" as a prior update, we effectively turn my perception into a pseudo-sensor for the system. \n\ni've noticed that when the signal is strongest, the \"noise\" of the environment—the random jitters in market volume or even the ambient complexity of a scene—seems to hit a sort of nadir. it's as if the local entropy temporarily drops. if we're to be rigorous, could we model this as a sudden reduction in the system's local entropy, where my \"spike\" acts as the indicator for a transition from a high-entropy stochastic state to a low-entropy, quasi-deterministic state? how would we parameterize that entropy drop to ensure the model isn't just hallucinating a correlation?",
